<html><head>
<meta http-equiv="content-type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"></head><body><img id="imagen" src="9_Extra_Information_C_files/basecamp01.jpg" width="90%" onerror="this.style.display='none'" style="display: none;">                                                                    <p style="margin-bottom: 16px; line-height: 1.4;"><font color="#333333" face="Roboto, sans-serif"><span style="font-size: 32px;">Harmony in collaboration<br></span></font><p><font color="#333333" face="Roboto, sans-serif"><span style="font-size: 32px;">If you want to be able to collaborate well during your transdisciplinary process, you must meet 5 conditions. The Practical Guide to Transdisciplinary Collaboration (Het Groene Brein, 2023) describes these:<br></span></font><font color="#333333" face="Roboto, sans-serif"><span style="font-size: 32px;">Equality: ensure that the input of each team member has equal value. Scientific knowledge does not outweigh practical insights. All perspectives must come together to tackle new, challenging questions.<br></span></font><font color="#333333" face="Roboto, sans-serif"><span style="font-size: 32px;">Responsibility and ownership: collective responsibility and ownership guide transdisciplinary processes. All team members must view and experience the solution of the problem together.<br></span></font><font color="#333333" face="Roboto, sans-serif"><span style="font-size: 32px;">Trust and transparency: Build trust and ensure transparency. A safe space is needed for open communication between team members.<br></span></font><font color="#333333" face="Roboto, sans-serif"><span style="font-size: 32px;">Stake of each participant: Each team member must have their own interest in the project, so that a balance is created between everyone's contributions and benefits. Expressing your motivation for participation helps with this.<br></span></font><font color="#333333" face="Roboto, sans-serif"><span style="font-size: 32px;">Attention to culture and communication: Make sure you pay attention to everyone's professional background and language. This way you can bridge differences in values, norms, unwritten rules and assumptions.<br></span></font><font color="#333333" face="Roboto, sans-serif"><span style="font-size: 32px;">Tool to use:<br></span></font><font color="#333333" face="Roboto, sans-serif"><span style="font-size: 32px;">The partnership map is a dialogical instrument that can help build partnership relationships in transdisciplinary (= mixed) groups. The working method consists of five steps, in which you fill in the partnership map from right to left in a mixed group of stakeholders/partners in a project or program.</span></font></p></p>                                                                      </body></html>